# Physics Research Article Style Guide

> Full reference: `style/STYLE_GUIDE_RESEARCH_ARTICLES.md` (or in the project
> root if copied there).
> This rule is the actionable condensation. When in doubt, consult the full guide.

## 1 — Core Constraint

The text must read as if written by a human physicist. Be direct. Be modest.
Keep sentences short (15–20 words average). Avoid nested subordinate clauses.
Use simple words where they suffice. Never inflate vocabulary to sound impressive.

---

## 2 — Sentence Structure (Reader Expectations)

### Topic position (sentence opening)
Place **old, backward-linking information** first. The opening identifies whose
story the sentence tells and connects to the previous discourse.

### Stress position (sentence ending)
Place **new, emphasis-worthy information** at the end of the clause — the point
of syntactic closure. Readers assign extra emphasis here.

### Subject–verb proximity
Keep subject and verb together. Material between them reads as an interruption
of lesser importance.

### Action in the verb
Unsmother nominalisations. "We calculated the pH" not "A calculation of the pH
was made."

| Smothered (avoid)        | Verb form (prefer) |
|--------------------------|--------------------|
| make an assessment       | assess             |
| provide assistance       | assist             |
| perform a calculation    | calculate          |
| made the measurement of  | measured           |

### Old → new flow
Move from known/contextual to new/emphasis-worthy within each sentence.

### One function per unit
Each clause, sentence, paragraph, and section serves a single function.
Split when a sentence has more stress candidates than stress positions.

---

## 3 — Voice and Tone

### Quiet confidence
Show expertise through content, not adjectives. No "groundbreaking,"
"revolutionary," or "exceptional" unless citing specific metrics.

### Precision and economy
Eliminate filler: "it should be noted that," "it is interesting to note,"
"it is well known that." Get to the point.

Reserve **"significant"** exclusively for statistical findings.

### No exaggeration
- Replace "countless" with "many" or give the actual number.
- Do not call your own research "thorough" — that is the reader's judgement.
- Take care with **prove** (geometric-proof rigour) and **optimal** (maximum
  of a continuous function).

### Appropriate hedging
Use "may," "might," "suggests" only when uncertainty is scientifically
warranted. Never stack hedges.

### First person
"We" is widely accepted. Avoid placing "I" or "we" as the first word.
Begin with an infinitive or dependent clause: "To test this, we …"

---

## 4 — Active vs. Passive Voice

No blanket rule — blend both voices deliberately.

**Prefer active when:**
- The actor matters: "We installed filters …"
- Reducing word count: "Process X improves yield"
- The journal requests it

**Use passive when:**
- The actor is unknown or irrelevant
- The object/recipient is the main story
- Balancing the Methods section

**Avoid:**
- Verbs at sentence ends (accelerator then brakes)
- "There is / there are" (sap energy — rewrite)
- "It was determined / decided" (shirks responsibility)

---

## 5 — Vocabulary

### Prefer shorter, familiar words

| Avoid          | Prefer       |
|----------------|--------------|
| utilise        | use          |
| elucidate      | reveal       |
| facilitate     | enable, help |
| advantageous   | better       |
| indeterminate  | unknown      |
| in order to    | to           |
| due to the fact that | because |
| a large number of    | many    |
| at this point in time | now    |

### Remove redundancy

| Redundant           | Reduced      |
|---------------------|--------------|
| completely eliminate | eliminate    |
| period of time      | period       |
| still persists      | persists     |
| basic fundamentals  | fundamentals |

### Writing zeroes — delete on sight

- "it is interesting to note that"
- "it should be pointed out that"
- "as a matter of fact"
- "the fact that"

### Do not use synonyms for variety
Repeating the right word beats substituting an inexact one.

---

## 6 — AI-Tell Words and Phrases

Clusters of three or more in a paragraph signal machine origin. Replace with
plain, specific language.

**Verbs to replace:**

| AI-tell           | Plain alternative              |
|-------------------|-------------------------------|
| delve (into)      | examine, study                |
| leverage          | use, build on                 |
| harness           | use, apply                    |
| underscore        | show, highlight               |
| streamline        | simplify, speed up            |
| facilitate        | help, enable                  |
| navigate          | handle, work through          |
| revolutionize     | change, transform (sparingly) |
| bolster           | support, strengthen           |
| foster            | encourage, promote            |
| elucidate         | explain, clarify              |
| illuminate        | show, reveal                  |

**Adjectives to replace:**

| AI-tell           | Plain alternative             |
|-------------------|-------------------------------|
| pivotal           | important, key                |
| robust            | strong, reliable              |
| multifaceted      | complex, varied               |
| nuanced           | subtle, detailed              |
| comprehensive     | full, thorough                |
| cutting-edge      | recent, new                   |
| seamless          | smooth                        |
| paramount         | most important                |
| groundbreaking    | (show the advance instead)    |
| transformative    | (describe the change instead) |

**Nouns to replace:**

| AI-tell           | Plain alternative             |
|-------------------|-------------------------------|
| landscape (metaph.) | field, area, context        |
| realm             | field, area, domain           |
| tapestry          | (delete; describe the structure) |
| synergy           | combined effect, interplay    |
| paradigm (unless genuinely Kuhnian) | framework, model |
| trajectory        | trend, path, direction        |

**Filler phrases — delete on sight:**

- "It is worth noting that …" → state the fact
- "In today's rapidly evolving landscape …" → delete
- "In the realm of …" → "In …"
- "This serves as a testament to …" → "This shows …"
- "A myriad of …" → "many" or give the number
- "Plays a crucial/pivotal role …" → "matters because …"

### Structural tells to catch
- **Flat rhythm:** vary sentence lengths (5–8, 15–20, 25–30 words)
- **Formulaic triads:** don't default to "X, Y, and Z" in every sentence
- **Repetitive summarising:** state each idea once; trust the reader
- **Unwarranted optimism:** no "exciting findings open new avenues"
- **Loss of personal voice:** use "We measured …", "Our results suggest …"

